.TH HSH 1 "September 2026" "Holberton School" "User Commands"


.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command line interpreter


.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh


.SH DESCRIPTION
hsh is a simple UNIX command line interpreter written in C
as part of the Holberton School curriculum.

.PP
It reads commands from standard input, finds the executable
using the PATH environment variable (or absolute/relative paths),
and executes it in a child process.

.PP
The shell works in both interactive and non-interactive modes.


.SH FEATURES
.IP \(bu 2
Displays a prompt ($ ) in interactive mode
.IP \(bu 2
Executes commands found in the PATH
.IP \(bu 2
Supports commands with arguments
.IP \(bu 2
Supports absolute and relative paths
.IP \(bu 2
Built-in commands: exit and env
.IP \(bu 2
Handles end-of-file (Ctrl+D)
.IP \(bu 2
Works with pipes and input redirection


.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell.

.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment variables.


.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
When run in a terminal, the shell shows a prompt and waits
for the user to type a command:

.PP
.nf
$ ./hsh
$ ls
$ pwd
$ ls -l /tmp
$ env
$ exit
.fi


.SH NON-INTERACTIVE MODE
Commands can be passed through a pipe or a file:

.PP
.nf
$ echo "ls" | ./hsh
$ echo "ls -l" | ./hsh
$ cat commands.txt | ./hsh
$ ./hsh < commands.txt
.fi

.PP
No prompt is displayed in non-interactive mode.


.SH ERROR HANDLING
If a command cannot be found, the shell prints:

.PP
.nf
./hsh: <line>: <command>: not found
.fi

.PP
and returns the exit status 127.


.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
The command was executed successfully.

.TP
127
The command was not found.

.TP
Other
The exit status of the last executed command.


.SH LIMITATIONS
This shell does not support:

.IP \(bu 2
Pipes (|)
.IP \(bu 2
Redirections (>, <, >>)
.IP \(bu 2
Command separators (;)
.IP \(bu 2
Logical operators (&&, ||)
.IP \(bu 2
Quotes or escape characters
.IP \(bu 2
Background processes (&)
.IP \(bu 2
Aliases, history, or job control


.SH EXAMPLES
Interactive:

.PP
.nf
$ ./hsh
$ /bin/ls
$ ls -la
$ env
$ exit
.fi

.PP
Non-interactive:

.PP
.nf
$ echo "pwd" | ./hsh
$ printf "ls\\nenv\\nexit\\n" | ./hsh
.fi


